1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:09,540
Slide 17 highlights WEBVIEW2 SECURITY SANDBOXING LIMITS. James, what happens when a web page talks directly to Windows?

2
00:00:09,940 --> 00:00:13,788
It is the most dangerous bridge in software engineering!

3
00:00:13,788 --> 00:00:18,392
In a normal browser, JavaScript cannot touch your local hard drive.

4
00:00:18,392 --> 00:00:23,960
But in WebView2, developers create a bridge to call local C# and Win32 functions!

5
00:00:24,360 --> 00:00:36,530
Look at Card 1: Cross-Site Injection. If an attacker tricks your web app into loading a malicious iframe, that iframe can send a `postMessage` to the host and execute local PowerShell commands!

6
00:00:36,930 --> 00:00:51,400
Look at Card 2: Local File Scheme Exploits. Attackers use `file:///C:/Users/` links to bypass web security origins and read your private SSH keys and browser passwords!

7
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:58,719
And Card 3: Unfiltered Native Interop. Never expose raw C# functions directly to JavaScript!

8
00:00:58,719 --> 00:01:04,020
You must wrap every bridge call with strict Pydantic validation schemas!

9
00:01:04,420 --> 00:01:10,060
If you don't validate, one rogue JavaScript payload can wipe your entire user directory!

10
00:01:10,460 --> 00:01:17,880
Bridging web technologies to operating systems requires ironclad cryptographic and schema validation gates.

